[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "ADR-2608190600"
  :adr/title "The plan deadlock has a way out, and the provider is told where it is"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-19"
  :adr/body
  "## What was still wrong

ADR-2608190200 closed the *consequence* of the plan deadlock: a resident tick
that blocked having only read now completes as a safe no-op instead of holding
the fleet's single slot. It said in its own text that the plan contract still
permitted a step no tool can satisfy, and that forbidding one was a separate
change. This is that change, and the earlier prevention turns out to have been
the weaker half of it.

The deadlock: `goal_complete` needs every plan step verified; a step is
verified only against a host execution receipt; a step that records a
conclusion executes no tool. A plan containing one can never complete.

What #116 added against it was a sentence in the `goal_plan` description --
finishing is not a step. That is advice. A provider that plans one anyway is
still stuck, and 326 of 461 stored resident runs were.

## Why it was unrecoverable rather than merely awkward

There IS a legal escape. `goal_plan` says \"create or revise\", so a provider
can plan again without the impossible step.

`clean-plan` reset every step to `:pending`. So the escape cost everything
already earned: a run that had verified step one by actually executing a tool
would have to execute it again. **The only way out of the trap was the most
expensive move available**, and the provider took the cheap one instead and
blocked. That is not the provider being unhelpful; it is the incentive the host
put in front of it.

## Decision

**Verification is carried across a revision.** A step keeps `:verified` when
its id, title and dependencies all match a step that earned it.

Carried, never granted. The receipt requirement in `goal_step_complete` is
untouched, and everything about the match is chosen so nothing can be inherited
that was not earned for that exact step: a renamed step comes back pending, a
re-pointed step comes back pending, and a step that was pending stays pending
however it is relabelled. Reverting the match to id alone -- the obvious
shortcut -- reddens
`revising-a-plan-cannot-grant-verification-it-did-not-earn`.

**The refusal names the blocker.** `goal_complete` used to restate its own rule:
a fully host-verified plan, one executed tool, a summary, evidence. It did not
say which was missing or for which step, so a provider that had planned an
impossible step learned only that it may not finish. The refusal now names the
unverified steps, says which of them no tool has executed for, says that such a
step can never carry a receipt, and says to call `goal_plan` again without it --
adding that verified steps survive the revision, because otherwise the advice
is one the provider has reason to ignore.

This admits nothing new. Only the explanation changed.

## The shape of the fix

Detection and recovery, not permission. The host still refuses; it now refuses
in a way that can be acted on, and the action it recommends is no longer
punitive. The safety net from ADR-2608190200 stays underneath for the case
where a provider blocks anyway.

## Verification

Three changes, each reverted alone, each reddening only its own tests:

  resetting every step on revision
    -> revising-a-plan-keeps-what-was-already-verified
  matching on id alone instead of id + title + dependencies
    -> revising-a-plan-cannot-grant-verification-it-did-not-earn
  restating the rule instead of naming the blocker
    -> a-refused-goal-says-which-step-blocks-it-and-what-to-do,
       a-refusal-with-nothing-executed-says-so

## Not measured yet

Whether providers actually take the way out. The store will say: a resident run
that revises its plan and completes leaves `plan/recorded` twice and a
`verifier/goal-passed`, where the same run today leaves a block. The outcome
tally from ADR-2608190400 is where that shows up, as `no-op` giving way to
`completed`."}]
